<section xml:id="sec-ch02-01-VectorValuedFunctions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Vector-Valued Functions</title>

  <note>
    <p>
      Think of a point tracing a path as it moves through <m>x y z</m>-space
      (for example, think of tracing the path a mosquito travels as it flies
      through a room). We can think of the curve traced by this point in two
      ways:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            The position of the point at a time <m>t</m>, given by
            <m>(x(t), y(t), z(t))</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The position of the point at a time <m>t</m>, given by the
            endpoint of the vector
            <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle x(t), y(t), z(t)\rangle</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </note>

  <!--\includegraphics[max width=\textwidth, center]{2025_08_04_3d11211490a50caa278ag-1}
      $\mathbf{r}\left(t_{0}\right)=\left\langle x\left(t_{0}\right), y\left(t_{0}\right), z\left(t_{0}\right)\right\rangle$
      \includegraphics[max width=\textwidth, center]{2025_08_04_3d11211490a50caa278ag-1(2)}
      $\mathbf{r}\left(t_{1}\right)=\left\langle x\left(t_{1}\right), y\left(t_{1}\right), z\left(t_{1}\right)\right\rangle$
      \includegraphics[max width=\textwidth, center]{2025_08_04_3d11211490a50caa278ag-1(1)}
      $\mathbf{r}\left(t_{2}\right)=\left\langle x\left(t_{2}\right), y\left(t_{2}\right), z\left(t_{2}\right)\right\rangle$-->

