     You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:<url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the left graph</url>, <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the middle graph</url>, <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the right graph</url>
  </p>
  <!--\caption*{From openstax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 2.2}-->

  <definition>
    <title>Vector-Valued Function</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        A vector-valued function is a function which assigns to each input
        <m>t</m> (the independent variable), an output of a vector.
        Mathematically, a vector-valued function can be written in the form
        <md>
          \vec{r}(t)=\langle f(t), g(t), h(t)\rangle, a \leq t \leq b
        </md> where each component of <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> is a function of
        <m>t</m> (each component is a dependent variable).
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      The domain of <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle f(t), g(t), h(t)\rangle</m> is the
      intersection of the domains of <m>f(t), g(t)</m>, and <m>h(t)</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-01-ex-5-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The graph of
        <m>\vec{r}(t)=\left\langle\cos (t), \sin (t), \frac{t}{10}\right\rangle</m>,
        <m>0 \leq t \leq 10</m> is shown below. If we don't restrict <m>t</m>,
        what is the domain of <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> ?
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-01-ex-5-1">
        <caption>The helix traced by the vector <m>\vec{r}(t)=\left\langle\cos (t), \sin (t), \frac{t}{10}\right\rangle</m>, for <m>t</m> from 0 to 10, in <m>xyz</m>-space.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-01-ex-5-1" variant="controls" width="90%" aspect="3:2"
          calcplot3d="type=spacecurve;spacecurve=curve;curvename=r_%7B2%7D;x=sin(t);y=cos(t);z=0.1t;visible=true;width=2;view=0;tmin=0;tmax=10;tsteps=500;color=rgb(30,136,229);showtrace=false;tval=1;constcol=true;twod=false;arrows=0;showpt=true;traceptsize=4;trace=true;vel=true;acc=true;veceqs=true;osc=false;k=false;showtorsion=false;repeat=false;bounce=false;dashed=false;tanline=false;dropcurtain=false;showtvector=false;shownvector=false;showbvector=false;showtnbeqs=false;showtnblabels=false;showoscplane=false;showrectplane=false;shownormplane=false;optimizecurve=true;maxjointangle=10&amp;type=vector;vector=%3Csin(1.7),%20cos(1.7),%20.17%3E;visible=false;color=rgb(216,27,96);size=2;initialpt=(0,0,0);dashed=undefined&amp;type=window;showfunnot=false;hsrmode=0;nomidpts=true;anaglyph=-1;center=4.941834621277992,2.85316954888546,1.8541019662496847,1;focus=0,0,0,1;up=0,0,2,1;transparent=true;alpha=140;twoviews=false;unlinkviews=false;axisextension=0.7;shownormals=false;shownormalsatpts=false;xaxislabel=x;yaxislabel=y;zaxislabel=z;edgeson=true;faceson=true;showbox=false;showaxes=true;showticks=true;perspective=true;centerxpercent=0.5;centerypercent=0.5;rotationsteps=30;autospin=true;xygrid=false;yzgrid=false;xzgrid=false;gridsonbox=true;gridplanes=false;gridcolor=rgb(128,128,128);lastaddedsurfaceactive=true;disabletrace=false;activefun=-1;xmin=-1;xmax=1;ymin=-1;ymax=1;zmin=-1;zmax=1;xscale=0.5;yscale=0.5;zscale=0.5;zcmin=-2;zcmax=2;xscalefactor=1;yscalefactor=1;zscalefactor=1;tracemode=0;keep2d=false;zoom=3.357333">
          <instructions>
            <p>
              Drag with the mouse to rotate the surface. You can open the plot in a new page from here: <url href="https://c3d.libretexts.org/CalcPlot3D/index.html?type=spacecurve;spacecurve=curve;curvename=r_%7B2%7D;x=sin(t);y=cos(t);z=0.1t;visible=true;width=2;view=0;tmin=0;tmax=10;tsteps=500;color=rgb(30,136,229);showtrace=false;tval=1;constcol=true;twod=false;arrows=0;showpt=true;traceptsize=4;trace=true;vel=true;acc=true;veceqs=true;osc=false;k=false;showtorsion=false;repeat=false;bounce=false;dashed=false;tanline=false;dropcurtain=false;showtvector=false;shownvector=false;showbvector=false;showtnbeqs=false;showtnblabels=false;showoscplane=false;showrectplane=false;shownormplane=false;optimizecurve=true;maxjointangle=10&amp;type=vector;vector=%3Csin(1.7),%20cos(1.7),%20.17%3E;visible=false;color=rgb(216,27,96);size=2;initialpt=(0,0,0);dashed=undefined&amp;type=window;showfunnot=false;hsrmode=0;nomidpts=true;anaglyph=-1;center=4.941834621277992,2.85316954888546,1.8541019662496847,1;focus=0,0,0,1;up=0,0,2,1;transparent=true;alpha=140;twoviews=false;unlinkviews=false;axisextension=0.7;shownormals=false;shownormalsatpts=false;xaxislabel=x;yaxislabel=y;zaxislabel=z;edgeson=true;faceson=true;showbox=false;showaxes=true;showticks=true;perspective=true;centerxpercent=0.5;centerypercent=0.5;rotationsteps=30;autospin=true;xygrid=false;yzgrid=false;xzgrid=false;gridsonbox=true;gridplanes=false;gridcolor=rgb(128,128,128);lastaddedsurfaceactive=true;disabletrace=false;activefun=-1;xmin=-1;xmax=1;ymin=-1;ymax=1;zmin=-1;zmax=1;xscale=0.5;yscale=0.5;zscale=0.5;zcmin=-2;zcmax=2;xscalefactor=1;yscalefactor=1;zscalefactor=1;tracemode=0;keep2d=false;zoom=3.357333" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the plot</url>
            </p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_08_04_3d11211490a50caa278ag-2-1-b.png">
              <shortdescription>The helix traced by the vector r of t with coordinates x = cosine of t, y = sine of t, z = t over 10, for t from 0 to 10, in xyz-space.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The helix traced by the vector <m>\vec{r}(t)=\left\langle\cos (t), \sin (t), \frac{t}{10}\right\rangle</m>, for <m>t</m> from 0 to 10, in <m>xyz</m>-space.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The domain of <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> is the intersection of the domains of
        its three components. Each of <m>\cos(t)</m>, <m>\sin(t)</m>, and
        <m>\frac{t}{10}</m> is defined for every real number <m>t</m>, so
        <md>
          \operatorname{dom}(\vec{r}) = (-\infty, \infty)
        </md>
        that is, the domain is all real numbers (and the graph becomes a helix
        spiraling upwards forever in both directions).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      If we write <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle f(t), g(t), h(t)\rangle</m> and don't
      specify an interval, assume that <m>t</m> can take any possible value in
      the domain of <m>\vec{r}(t)</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <p>
      The output vector of a vector-valued function doesn't have to be three
      dimensional. For example, the function
      <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle t, -\frac{t}{2}+1\rangle,0 \leq t \leq 2</m>
      traces the graph of <m>y=-\frac{x}{2}+1</m> on the interval
      <m>[0,2]</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-01-line-segment">
      <caption>This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_04_3d11211490a50caa278ag-2-1.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-01-line-segment" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
        <instructions>
          <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
        </instructions>
        <static>
          <image source="/images/2025_08_04_3d11211490a50caa278ag-2-1.png">
            <shortdescription>The line segment traced by the vector r of t with coordinates x = t, y = negative t over 2, plus 1, for t from 0 to 2, in the xy-plane.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>The line segment traced by the vector <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle t, -\frac{t}{2}+1\rangle</m>, for <m>t</m> from 0 to 2, in the <m>xy</m>-plane.</p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </static>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <p>
      Since vector-valued functions trace a curve as time passes by, we can
      talk about the orientation (or direction) in which the curve is traced.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-01-ex-5-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each of the following planar curves, write a vector-valued
        function which traces them with the specified orientation. Moreover,
        graph the given curves using arrows to indicate the orientation.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              A circle of radius 1 (traced once) oriented counterclockwise,
              starting at <m>(1,0)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The graph of <m>y=x^{2}-1</m>, on the interval <m>[-1,1]</m>,
              traced from left to right (relative to the <m>x</m>-axis).
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The function
        <md>
          \vec{r}(t) = \langle \cos(t), \sin(t) \rangle, \quad 0 \leq t \leq 2\pi
        </md>
        traces the circle of radius 1 centered at the origin, since
        <m>x(t)^2+y(t)^2 = \cos^2(t)+\sin^2(t) = 1</m>. It starts at
        <m>\vec{r}(0) = \langle 1,0 \rangle</m>, passes through <m>(0,1)</m>
        at <m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}</m>, through <m>(-1,0)</m> at <m>t=\pi</m>, and
        through <m>(0,-1)</m> at <m>t=\frac{3\pi}{2}</m> <ndash/> a
        counterclockwise motion <ndash/> and returns to <m>(1,0)</m> at
        <m>t=2\pi</m>, tracing the circle exactly once. To graph it, draw the
        unit circle with the arrows on it pointing counterclockwise.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-01-ex-5-2a">
        <caption>This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to Example 5.2-1.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-01-ex-5-2a" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/Example-5.2-1.png">
              <shortdescription>The unit circle oriented counter-clockwise.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The unit circle oriented counter-clockwise.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Letting <m>x=t</m>, the point on the graph above <m>x</m> is
        <m>(t, t^2-1)</m>, so
        <md>
          \vec{r}(t) = \langle t, t^2-1 \rangle, \quad -1 \leq t \leq 1
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Since <m>x(t)=t</m> increases as <m>t</m> increases, the parabola is
        traced from left to right: starting at <m>(-1,0)</m>, down to the
        vertex <m>(0,-1)</m>, and up to <m>(1,0)</m>. To graph it, draw this
        piece of the upward parabola with the arrows on it pointing from left
        to right.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-01-ex-5-2b">
        <caption>This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to Example 5.2-2.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-01-ex-5-2b" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/Example-5.2-2.png">
              <shortdescription>The parabola y equals x squared minus one, from x equals -1 to x equals 1, traversed from left to right.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The parabola <m>y=x^{2}-1</m>, from <m>x=-1</m> to <m>x=1</m>, traversed from left to right.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Generally if you want to quickly parametrize a function <m>f(x)</m> on
      the interval <m>x\in [a,b]</m> from left to right, then you can use
      <m>\vec{r}(t) = \langle t, f(t) \rangle</m> for <m>t\in [a,b]</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-01-ex-5-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For the following planar curve, write a vector-valued function which
        traces it with the specified orientation. Moreover, graph the given
        curve using arrows to indicate the orientation.
      </p>

      <p>
        The graph of <m>y=x^{2}-4 x+3</m>, on the interval <m>[1,5]</m>,
        traced from right to left (relative to the <m>x</m>-axis)
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        To trace the graph from right to left we need the <m>x</m>-coordinate
        to decrease as <m>t</m> increases and there multiple options to
        achieve this. Here are three:
      </p>

      <p>
        <alert>Option A</alert> Let <m>x = 5-t</m>. Then
        <md>
          y = x^2-4x+3 = (5-t)^2-4(5-t)+3 = t^2-6t+8
        </md>
        and <m>x=5-t</m> runs from 5 down to 1 as <m>t</m> runs from 0 to 4.
        Hence
        <md>
          \vec{r}(t) = \langle 5-t, t^2-6t+8 \rangle, \quad 0 \leq t \leq 4
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Indeed, <m>\vec{r}(0) = \langle 5,8 \rangle</m> and
        <m>\vec{r}(4) = \langle 1,0 \rangle</m>, so the curve starts at the
        right endpoint <m>(5,8)</m> and ends at the left endpoint
        <m>(1,0)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <alert>Option B</alert> Let <m>x = 6-t</m>. Then
        <md>
          y = x^2-4x+3 = (6-t)^2-4(6-t)+3 = t^2-8t+15
        </md>
        and <m>x=6-t</m> runs from 5 down to 1 as <m>t</m> runs from 1 to 5.
        Hence
        <md>
          \vec{r}(t) = \langle 6-t, t^2-8t+15 \rangle, \quad 1 \leq t \leq 5
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Indeed, <m>\vec{r}(1) = \langle 5,8 \rangle</m> and
        <m>\vec{r}(5) = \langle 1,0 \rangle</m>, so the curve starts at the
        right endpoint <m>(5,8)</m> and ends at the left endpoint
        <m>(1,0)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <alert>Option C</alert> Let <m>x = -t</m>. Then
        <md>
          y = x^2-4x+3 = (-t)^2-4(-t)+3 = t^2+4t+3
        </md>
        and <m>x=-t</m> runs from 5 down to 1 as <m>t</m> runs from
        <m>-5</m> to <m>-1</m>. Hence
        <md>
          \vec{r}(t) = \langle -t, t^2+4t+3 \rangle, \quad -5 \leq t \leq -1
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Indeed, <m>\vec{r}(-5) = \langle 5,8 \rangle</m> and
        <m>\vec{r}(-1) = \langle 1,0 \rangle</m>, so the curve starts at the
        right endpoint <m>(5,8)</m> and ends at the left endpoint
        <m>(1,0)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        To graph it, draw the piece of the upward parabola with vertex
        <m>(2,-1)</m> and <m>x</m>-intercepts <m>(1,0)</m> and <m>(3,0)</m>,
        between <m>x=1</m> and <m>x=5</m>, with the arrows on it pointing from
        right to left.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-01-ex-5-3">
        <caption>This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to Example 5.3.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch02-01-ex-5-3" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/Example-5.3.png">
              <shortdescription>The parabola y equals x squared minus 4 x plus three, from x equals 1 to x equals 5, traversed from right to left.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The parabola <m>y=x^{2}-4x+3</m>, from <m>x=1</m> to <m>x=5</m>, traversed from right to left.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Generally if you want to quickly parametrize a function <m>f(x)</m> on
      the interval <m>x\in [a,b]</m> from right to left, then you can use on
      of the following.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\vec{r}(t) = \langle b-t, f(b-t) \rangle</m> for
            <m>t\in [0,b-a]</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\vec{r}(t) = \langle b+a-t, f(b+a-t) \rangle</m> for
            <m>t\in [a,b]</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\vec{r}(t) = \langle -t, f(-t) \rangle</m> for
            <m>t\in [-b,-a]</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <remark>
    <title>Parametric Equations for a Line</title>

    <p>
      The components <m>f(t), g(t)</m>, and <m>h(t)</m> of
      <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle f(t), g(t), h(t)\rangle</m> are also called
      parametric equations.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition>
    <title>Line in Parametric Form</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        A line in <m>x y z</m>-space (or in 2D space) can be defined as a
        vector-valued function by the equation
        <md>
          <mrow>\vec{r}(t) \amp =\vec{r}_{0}+t \vec{v}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp =\left\langle x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0}\right\rangle+t\langle a, b, c\rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp =\left\langle x_{0}+a t, y_{0}+b t, z_{0}+c t\right\rangle</mrow>
        </md> where <m>-\infty \lt t \lt \infty, P_{0}\left(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0}\right)</m>
        is any point on the line, and <m>\vec{v}=\langle a, b, c\rangle</m>
        (the direction vector) gives you the change in each coordinate.
      </p>

      <p>
        The equations <m>f(t)=x_{0}+a t, g(t)=y_{0}+b t</m>, and
        <m>h(t)=z_{0}+c t</m> are the parametric equations that define the
        line.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <note>
    <p>
      Geometrically, the definition above says that you take the line
      containing <m>\vec{v}</m> (represented by <m>t \vec{v}</m> ), and shift
      it so it passes through the endpoint of <m>\vec{r}_{0}</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch02-01-line-parametric">
      <caption>A line in <m>xyz</m>-space defined by the position vector <m>\vec{r}_{0}</m> and a direction vector <m>\vec{v}</m>, illustrating the parametric form: <m>\vec{r}(t)=\vec{r}_{0}+t\vec{v}</m>.</caption>
