              <p>A line in <m>xyz</m>-space defined by the position vector <m>\vec{r}_{0}</m> and a direction vector <m>\vec{v}</m>, illustrating the parametric form: <m>\vec{r}(t)=\vec{r}_{0}+t\vec{v}</m>.</p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </static>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-01-ex-5-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find both the vector equation and the parametric equations of the
        line through <m>P(1,2,3)</m> in the direction of the vector
        <m>\vec{v}=\langle 3,2,1 \rangle</m>. Does the point
        <m>\left(\frac{5}{4},\frac{13}{6},\frac{37}{12}\right)</m> lie on
        this line?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Using <m>P(1,2,3)</m> as the point on the line and
        <m>\vec{v}=\langle 3,2,1 \rangle</m> as the direction vector, the
        vector equation of the line is
        <md>
          \vec{r}(t) = \vec{r}_0+t\vec{v} = \langle 1,2,3 \rangle + t\langle 3,2,1 \rangle = \langle 1+3t, 2+2t, 3+t \rangle, \quad -\infty \lt t \lt \infty
        </md>
        and the parametric equations are
        <md>
          x = 1+3t, \quad y = 2+2t, \quad z = 3+t
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        For the point
        <m>\left(\frac{5}{4},\frac{13}{6},\frac{37}{12}\right)</m> to lie on
        the line, all three parametric equations must be satisfied by the same
        value of <m>t</m>. From the first equation:
        <md>
          1+3t = \frac{5}{4} \Rightarrow 3t = \frac{1}{4} \Rightarrow t = \frac{1}{12}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Checking this value in the other two equations:
        <md>
          y = 2+2 \cdot \frac{1}{12} = 2+\frac{1}{6} = \frac{13}{6}, \quad z = 3+\frac{1}{12} = \frac{37}{12}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Both match, so yes: the point lies on the line (it is reached at
        <m>t = \frac{1}{12}</m>).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Given a line <m>\vec{r}(t)=\vec{r}_{0}+t \vec{v}</m>, since
      <m>\vec{v}</m> represents the direction of the line, we can compare
      lines by comparing their direction vectors.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch02-01-ex-5-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether the following pair of lines are parallel, intersect
        at a single point, or are skew. If they are parallel, determine
        whether they are the same line. If they intersect at a single point,
        determine the point of intersection.
        <md>
          <mrow>\amp \vec{r}_{1}(t)=\langle 3,2,1\rangle+\langle 1,2,3 \rangle t,\quad -\infty \lt t \lt \infty</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \vec{r}_{2}(t)=\langle 1,2,3\rangle+\langle 3,2,1 \rangle t,\quad -\infty \lt t \lt \infty</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The direction vectors are <m>\vec{v}_1 = \langle 1,2,3 \rangle</m>
        for the first line and <m>\vec{v}_2 = \langle 3,2,1 \rangle</m> for
        the second. If the lines were parallel there would be a scalar
        <m>c</m> with <m>c\vec{v}_1 = \vec{v}_2</m>; comparing components
        gives <m>c=3</m>, <m>c=1</m>, and <m>c=\frac{1}{3}</m> respectively,
        which is impossible. Hence the lines are not parallel (and in
        particular they are not the same line).
      </p>

      <p>
        It remains to determine whether the lines intersect at a single point
        or are skew. An intersection point may be reached by each line at a
        different time, so we rename the parameter of the second line and
        look for values <m>t</m> and <m>s</m> with
        <m>\vec{r}_1(t) = \vec{r}_2(s)</m>. Comparing components gives the
        system
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>3+t = 1+3s</mrow>
          <mrow>2+2t = 2+2s</mrow>
          <mrow>1+3t = 3+s</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The second equation of the system gives
        <m>2t = 2s \Rightarrow t = s</m>. Substituting into the first
        equation:
        <md>
          3+t = 1+3t \Rightarrow 2 = 2t \Rightarrow t = 1, \quad s = 1
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Since we only used the first two equations, we must check these
        values in the third: <m>1+3(1) = 4</m> and <m>3+1 = 4</m>, so the
        third equation is also satisfied. Hence the system is consistent and
        the lines intersect at the single point
        <md>
          \vec{r}_1(1) = \langle 3+1, 2+2, 1+3 \rangle = \langle 4,4,4 \rangle
        </md>
        that is, at the point <m>(4,4,4)</m>. (Indeed,
        <m>\vec{r}_2(1) = \langle 1+3, 2+2, 3+1 \rangle = \langle 4,4,4 \rangle</m>
        as well.)
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
